================================================================================
THIRD-PARTY MATERIAL INCLUDED IN THIS REPOSITORY
================================================================================

data/cvd_colormaps.txt contains three colour tables that were not created
for this project. All three are CC0 1.0 Universal public-domain
dedications, so no legal restriction applies to their inclusion,
redistribution or modification. Their authors ask only that credit be
given where they are used, and this section gives it.

viridis, magma
    Stefan van der Walt and Nathaniel Smith, with Eric Firing for
    viridis. Released to the public domain under CC0 through the BIDS
    colormap project (https://bids.github.io/colormap/) and distributed
    with matplotlib.

cividis
    Nunez JR, Anderton CR, Renslow RS (2018). "Optimizing colormaps with
    consideration for color vision deficiency to enable accurate
    interpretation of scientific data." PLoS ONE 13(7): e0199239.
    https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0199239
    Published open access under the CC0 public-domain dedication.

These three are included, rather than approximated by a ramp generated
here, because they encode a MEASURED perceptual property - monotone
perceived lightness, and readability under the common forms of colour
vision deficiency - that this project has no instrument to reproduce or
verify. Substituting a hand-rolled approximation and describing it as
colour-vision-deficiency-safe would be a perceptual claim with nothing
behind it.

The tables are regenerated by tools/extract_cvd_colormaps.py, which
records the matplotlib version it drew them from in the data file's own
header, so a reader can reproduce them rather than trust them.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

data/etopo_10min_surface.mat is a reduced form of a NOAA relief model. It
is a work of the United States federal government, which is not subject
to domestic copyright protection under 17 U.S.C. Section 105, so no
licence restriction applies to its inclusion or redistribution. Credit is
given because the producers ask for it and because a reader is entitled
to know what the terrain under a map actually is.

ETOPO 2022
    NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information (2022). ETOPO
    2022 15 Arc-Second Global Relief Model. NOAA National Centers for
    Environmental Information. https://doi.org/10.25921/fd45-gt74
    Described in: Macfarlane et al. (2025), "The Earth Topography 2022
    (ETOPO 2022) global DEM dataset", Earth System Science Data 17,
    1835-1857. https://doi.org/10.5194/essd-17-1835-2025

WHAT WAS DONE TO IT, so that nobody mistakes this file for the product:
the ICE SURFACE variant at 60 arc-seconds was reduced to 10 arc-minutes
by the unweighted mean of each 10x10 block and rounded to whole metres.
Elevations are heights above the EGM2008 geoid (EPSG:3855), not above the
ellipsoid, and horizontal coordinates are cell-centred WGS 84. The
reduction attenuates extremes - the global range narrows from
-10251..6344 m to -10082..6259 m - and a block straddling a coast
averages land and sea, so small islands vanish from it. Nothing in this
toolbox derives a land mask from this file; coastlines are vector data
from GSHHG or Natural Earth, which are not redistributed here.

The ICE SURFACE variant was chosen over BEDROCK deliberately. Coastlines
in Antarctica and Greenland trace the ice front, so a bedrock basemap
would paint the interior of the coastline below sea level: measured at
80S 100W, the surface is +2080 m and the bed is -1158 m. The two layers
of one map would contradict each other. The bedrock variant is fully
supported as an input; it is simply not what ships as the default.

The file is regenerated by tools/makeTopographySample.m from the
original, which records its provenance inside the .mat itself, so a
reader can reproduce it rather than trust it.

Everything else in this repository is original work under the MIT licence
above.
